// A volatile write is lifted out of the branch that guards it, like a
// relaxed one.
//
// Both arms write 1 to y, so lifting merges the two writes into one that the
// branch no longer guards, and the write loses its dependency on the read of
// x. Every path still performs one volatile write of 1 to y, which is all
// volatile keeps: C and C++ allow the same for volatile accesses, and GCC
// and Clang hoist such a store out of the branch from -O2.
// relaxed-write-lifted.lit is the same program without the annotations.
//
// Volatile keeps an access from being elided; see
// volatile-store-not-elided.lit. A write that must stay ordered after the
// read its branch tests needs a release store or an acquire read.
//
// The read of x is volatile so that it cannot be forwarded from x := 0. A
// forwarded read drops its dependency too, and the assertion counts a
// forwarded read as executed, so forwarding would remove (3,5) from .dp
// without any lifting.
x := 0;
y := 0;
r1 :v= x;
if (r1 = 0) {
  y :v= 1;
} else {
  y :v= 1;
}
%% allow ((3,5) ∉ .dp) []
